You absolutely can! If the Authorization page logic is working perfectly for you, the cleanest and most consistent approach is to simply reuse that exact same backend Java logic for the Status screen.
By doing this, the Java server becomes the single source of truth for both screens, ensuring they always match perfectly.
Here is exactly how to pipe that same backend logic into your Status screen.
### 1. Update the Backend (VoucherQueryService.java)
We need to add your existing calculateTimeSinceLastAction(batchId) method to the query that feeds the Status screen (all-requests-summary).
Find the method in VoucherQueryService.java that handles /api/voucher/all-requests-summary (usually named getAllRequestsSummary or getBatchesSummary).
Inside the RowMapper (the section where you put data into the Map<String, Object>), add this exact line:
**Add this line into your mapping:**
```java
    // ... existing mappings ...
    map.put("batchStatus", rs.getString("BATCH_STATUS"));
    map.put("currentOrder", rs.getInt("WORKFLOW_ORDER"));
    
    // ---> ADD THIS LINE <---
    // Reuse the exact same function the Authorization screen uses
    map.put("timeSinceLastAction", calculateTimeSinceLastAction(rs.getString("BATCH_ID")));
    
    return map;

```
### 2. Update the Frontend Modal State
Now that the backend is sending the pre-calculated string (e.g., "2 hr 15 min"), we just need to catch it and display it in the modal.
**File:** VoucherPostingStatus.js
**Step A:** Add a state variable to hold the new string at the top of the component:
```javascript
  const [viewingBatchRequestCount, setViewingBatchRequestCount] = useState(0);
  // Add this new state
  const [viewingBatchTimeSinceLastAction, setViewingBatchTimeSinceLastAction] = useState("");

```
**Step B:** Update the handleOpenViewModal function to capture the string when the user clicks "View & actions":
```javascript
  const handleOpenViewModal = async (row) => {
    setViewingBatchId(row.batchId);
    setViewingBatchCreatorId(row.creatorId);
    setViewingBatchState(row.currentStatus || "");
    setViewingBatchRawStatus(row.batchStatus || "");
    setViewingBatchRequestDate(row.requestDate);
    setViewingBatchRequestCount(row.requestCount || 0);
    setViewingBatchOrder(row.currentOrder);
    
    // ---> ADD THIS LINE <---
    setViewingBatchTimeSinceLastAction(row.timeSinceLastAction || "");
    
    setIsViewModalOpen(true);
    // ... rest of the function ...

```
### 3. Display it in the Timeline
Finally, we can remove the frontend calculation entirely and just print the string from the backend.
**Scroll down to the Big Workflow inside your DialogContent and find this block:**
```javascript
                            {isCurrent && viewingBatchState !== "REJECTED" && (
                              <Typography
                                variant="caption"
                                display="block"
                                color={statusFlag === 'ESCALATED' ? "warning.main" : "text.secondary"}
                                fontWeight="bold"
                                sx={{ mt: 0.5 }}
                              >
                                Pending Since: {calculateTimeElapsed(viewingBatchRequestDate)}
                              </Typography>
                            )}

```
**Replace it completely with this:**
```javascript
                            {isCurrent && viewingBatchState !== "REJECTED" && (
                              <Typography
                                variant="caption"
                                display="block"
                                color={statusFlag === 'ESCALATED' ? "warning.main" : "text.secondary"}
                                fontWeight="bold"
                                sx={{ mt: 0.5 }}
                              >
                                Pending Since: {viewingBatchTimeSinceLastAction || "Just now"}
                              </Typography>
                            )}

```
*(Note: You can also safely delete the calculateTimeElapsed function from the top of VoucherPostingStatus.js entirely, as React no longer needs to do any math!)*
